A||||4||PaUL||Steering Rack Ratios
Z||000000||PaUL||07-22-99||02:53 PM||||I read that the steering racks on the 84-87 fieros came with different ratios depending on year and model. The GT's came with a higher ratio rack which made the steering quicker but increased steering effort. Are these rack interchangeable between years and models?
Z||000001||ezsurf||07-22-99||08:19 PM||||PaUL,<br>I'd like to know where you read this.  I've been wondering about the steering rack ever since I bought the car.  As soon as I bought the car I basically drove it to my dad's garage and rebuilt the engine.  I've only driven the car 40 miles.  At speeds around 40 mph or less the car seemed fine.  But when I ran about 60-65 mph, the steering was very twitchy.  As hard as it was for me to believe, the car was swaying.  I checked the front suspension asap.  The front shocks were shot.  So, I changed them and put urethane bushings on the front swaybar and end links.  I haven't had it on the road since April, so I don't know how these changes have effected the handling.  I would like to know how many turns to lock the steering unit is.  The more the better in my case.  The less turns to lock the more sensitive the steering will be.  I want to keep this car, but I can't do that if it's going to be headed for the ditch every two seconds.  I've even been considering some kind of steering damper if I can find one.  I'm going to wait till I get it back on the road, see how it handles, and then go from there.  I've got an 86 SE with a V-6.  Tell me how your car handles at higher speeds.
Z||000002||PaUL||07-22-99||08:40 PM||||I got this info from lots of sources. I first read about the different ratio steering racks in an IRM catalogue. They had a Indy pacecar rack which had an even higher ratio than on the GTs. I also read about it in a book I have called "Fiero, Potent Midengined Sportscar". It said that the non GTs have a lower ratio rack because of the steering effort. Non GT buyers were presumed to be non sportscar drivers by GM, who wouldnt stand for a car with such a high steering effort. 
Z||000003||PaUL||07-22-99||09:01 PM||||My fiero did feel twitchy at high speeds and there was a slight pull to one side when braking. I ignored this problem for years until I noticed one of my tie-rod ends was loose when I was changing the front ball joints. After I fixed the tierod end and installed the new ball joints, my car felt incredibly solid at high speed. No pulling or twitchiness, it was like getting a new car.
Z||000004||lowCG||07-23-99||01:44 AM||||I've been holding onto a low mile Opel rack that will bolt into my Fiero with a few modifications,well worth the effort to swap,with much higher build/metals quality .I think it was 4 turns,lock to lock in the Opel,worked just fine.Also,when you increase the ratio,you lose some feedback,an element I savor in driving.
